<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!--
      T-W2-I6-THEME / T-WALLPAPER — GuardTalkFrameworkBrandOverlay config.
      Points the framework at the GuardTalk brand. The default wallpaper is
      now wired (T-WALLPAPER / FR-2) via the default_wallpaper drawable
      override in res/drawable-*/. Remaining logo-drawable asset paths
      activate when the operator delivers the rest of the brand kit (see
      vendor/guardtalk/branding/README.md gap register).

      Only framework-defined, documented config keys are referenced. No
      hidden/private resource ids (Law 4 / Law 8).
    -->

    <!--
      T-WALLPAPER (FR-2) — Default wallpaper wiring.

      AOSP does NOT expose a `config_default_wallpaper` config key. The
      default static wallpaper is resolved in
      frameworks/base/core/java/android/app/WallpaperManager.java:3438 via the
      hardcoded reference `R.drawable.default_wallpaper`, then loaded through
      `context.getResources().openRawResource(defaultResId)` (line 3450). The
      framework ships `default_wallpaper.png` under
      frameworks/base/core/res/res/drawable-nodpi/ (and -sw600dp-nodpi /
      -sw720dp-nodpi for tablets).

      Because this RRO targets `android` (targetPackage="android",
      isStatic="true", priority="999" — see AndroidManifest.xml), a
      `default_wallpaper.png` placed in this overlay's drawable buckets
      overrides the framework drawable at resource resolution time. There is
      no config-string or string-array to set; the drawable name itself is
      the override key.

      Drawable bucket placement (mirrors AOSP's density layout):
        res/drawable-nodpi/default_wallpaper.png     1440x3120 (universal fallback)
        res/drawable-xxhdpi/default_wallpaper.png    1080x2424 (Pixel 9 / tokay)
        res/drawable-xxxhdpi/default_wallpaper.png   1280x2856 (Pixel 9 Pro XL)

      Sources copied verbatim from vendor/guardtalk/branding/wallpapers/
      (wallpaper_guardtalk_default_nodpi.png, _default.png, _default_proxl.png).

      Effect: WallpaperManager.openDefaultWallpaper(ctx, FLAG_SYSTEM) returns
      the GuardTalk logo (obsidian) stream on first boot AND after every
      `wallpaperManager.clearWallpaper()` call, which is the path taken by
      WallpaperUpdateReceiver.updateWallpaper() on
      ACTION_DEVICE_CUSTOMIZATION_READY (e.g. factory reset). §9 (first boot)
      and §11 (factory reset re-apply) are both satisfied by the same
      resource-override mechanism.
    -->

    <!--
      T-BRAND-001 scoping note — the following framework config entries
      reference "grapheneos" but are intentionally NOT overridden here:

      - config_locationProviderPackageNames: contains the package id
        `app.grapheneos.networklocation` — this is the *installed package
        name* of the NetworkLocation app, not a brand string. Renaming it
        here would break package resolution at runtime (Law 8 determinism /
        Law 6 minimal footprint). The NetworkLocation app's own package id
        is owned by packages/apps/NetworkLocation and is out of scope for a
        brand-string sweep.
      - config_httpsTimeUrls: contains the upstream OS HTTPS time-check
        hostname (`https://time.<upstream-os>.org/...`) — this is the
        *actual* HTTPS time-check hostname operated as infrastructure.
        Replacing the bare brand string would point the device at a
        non-existent host and break network time sync. The operator must
        stand up a guardtalk.io mirror before this can be repointed; tracked
        as an infra deliverable, not a brand-string change.
      - config_defaultQrCodeComponent: contains
        `app.grapheneos.camera/...` — installed package/component id, same
        rationale as config_locationProviderPackageNames.

      User-visible brand strings that DO surface in framework UI (e.g.
      deprecated_abi_message_grapheneos) are overridden in strings.xml.
    -->
    <!--
      T-THEME-DARK (FR-1) — Force GuardTalkOS to dark theme only on first boot.

      config_defaultNightMode = 2 (MODE_NIGHT_YES). UiModeManagerService.onStart()
      (frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/UiModeManagerService.java)
      reads this integer as the initial value of mNightMode for every user, and
      updateNightModeFromSettingsLocked() uses it as the fallback when
      Settings.Secure.UI_NIGHT_MODE is unset (i.e. fresh boot / new user). Setting
      it to 2 guarantees the very first UI configuration of every user is
      UI_MODE_NIGHT_YES, so SystemUI, Settings, SetupWizard and all framework
      surfaces render dark from first boot.

      This is reinforced by the upstream framework default
      config_lockDayNightMode = true (see frameworks/base core config.xml:890),
      which gates UiModeManager.setNightMode[Activated]() behind the
      signature|privileged|role permission MODIFY_DAY_NIGHT_MODE. Settings
      (com.android.settings) does NOT hold that permission
      (frameworks/base/data/etc/privapp-permissions-platform.xml:705), so the
      Settings dark-theme toggle + scheduling rows are functionally inert:
      they still render but every setNightMode[Activated]() call is rejected
      by UiModeManagerService with "Night mode locked, requires
      MODIFY_DAY_NIGHT_MODE permission".

      Known gap (out of scope, documented for the Architect): com.android.shell
      IS granted MODIFY_DAY_NIGHT_MODE
      (frameworks/base/data/etc/privapp-permissions-platform.xml:279), so
      `adb shell cmd uimode night no` can still flip the mode at runtime. A
      system-property / init.rc lock would be required to fully close this
      path; that is T-BT-FULL / Wave-4 territory and explicitly out of scope
      for T-THEME-DARK (no init.rc edits).
    -->
    <integer name="config_defaultNightMode" translatable="false">2</integer>
</resources>
